ef 17592186045417] [17592186124798 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the domain of the function  $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x-y).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124798 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8d83dba6-238e-5ccd-970d-da3225e73ce3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124798 #edb/ref 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The argument of the arccosine has values between $-1$ and $1.$\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-1\\le x-y\\le 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-1\\le x-y\\le 1}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124801 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124801 #edb/ref 1069 "q-158197" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124801 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $g(x, y) = \\frac{1}{x - y}$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124801 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "036420cb-4253-5cbd-8d56-15a7f64df030" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125608 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125608 #edb/ref 1061 41878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125608 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fc31f470-ef05-57c5-83e5-74d5c5660924" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125608 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125609 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125609 #edb/ref 1061 41879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125609 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7be0d289-aa76-565b-b554-0640ab33fae2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125609 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125610 #edb/ref 1061 41882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125610 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a7e9f458-f87e-5aa2-a10b-9d3c56318005" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125610 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125611 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125611 #edb/ref 1061 41892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125611 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7cb633e3-6b78-502b-8402-00d3cc9b88b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125611 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125614 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125614 #edb/ref 1061 41906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125614 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b55f77a9-8540-5fb3-b460-fd3dba21c5e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125614 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125620 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125620 #edb/ref 1061 42402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125620 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8bf79d7f-8d55-5330-9913-9bcd94008524" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125620 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125621 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125621 #edb/ref 1061 42403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125621 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "177400d3-2550-582c-ad75-57483a6bbe30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125621 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125865 #edb/ref 1064 "Another way of visualizing a two-variable function is to draw its graph as a **surface** in a three-dimensional coordinate system.\n\nFor example, consider the function\n\n$$z = f(x,y) = x^2 +y^2, \\quad (x,y)\\in D$$\n\nwhere the (restricted) domain is given by the unit disc\n\n$$D = \\big\\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\: : \\: x^2+y^2 \\leq 1 \\big\\}.$$\n\nThe graph of $f$ is a surface that consists of all the points with coordinates\n\n$$(x, \\, y, \\, f(x,y)), \\quad (x,y) \\in D,$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/a3/a3c9c7eca49a0f828e53c0775afac16335033c1a8881367e49098bf7125ee2e6.png)\n\nNotice that range of $f$ is represented by the interval $R=[0,1]$ on the vertical $z$-axis. It consists of the possible $z$-coordinates of the points on our surface." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125865 #edb/ref 1065 6920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125865 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9bf49e76-e25e-5191-ad7f-95028f749c37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125865 #edb/ref 1067 "The Graph of a Two-Variable Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125868 #edb/ref 1064 "A function $f(x,y)$ of two variables is a rule that maps each pair of numbers $(x,y)\\in D$ to a unique real number. Under this notation, $D\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2$ is the **domain** of $f.$\n\nFor instance, consider the following two-variable function: \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\dfrac{\\sin(xy)}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this function by substituting concrete pairs of numbers for $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n- Let's evaluate $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2, \\pi).$ To do that, we substitute $x=2$ and $y=\\pi$ into the function expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,\\pi ) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(xy)}{\\sqrt{x-1}}{|}_{(2,\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot \\pi )}{\\sqrt{2-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(2,\\pi)$ returned a unique real number, this means that $(2,\\pi)$ belongs to the domain of $f,$ i.e., $(2,\\pi)\\in D.$\n\n- Let's now try to evaluate $f(x,y)$ at the point $(0, \\pi).$ To do that, we substitute $x=0$ and $y=\\pi$ into the function expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,\\pi ) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(xy)}{\\sqrt{x-1}}{|}_{(0,\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(0\\cdot \\pi )}{\\sqrt{0-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined over the real numbers.\n\nSince $f(0,\\pi)$ did not return a unique *real* number, this means that $(0,\\pi)\\notin D.$\n\nWe can also have functions of three variables $f(x,y,z),$ functions of four variables, $f(x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4),$ etc. There is no limit to the number of variables a function can have. Functions with more than one variable are called **multivariable functions.**" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125868 #edb/ref 1065 7034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125868 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8d127fec-12ff-5644-b095-33e9b94a52d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125868 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125869 #edb/ref 1064 "The **domain** of a multivariable function is the set of points where the function is defined. \n\nFor example, consider the function \n\n$$\nz = f(x,y) =  \\sqrt{1 -x^2 -y^2}.\n$$\n\nThe function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the expression under the square root is non-negative. As a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1 - x^2 - y^2 \\geq 0 \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx^2 + y^2 \\leq 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, x^2 + y^2 \\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nGeometrically, the domain of our function is a disc of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane, as shown on the left diagram below.\n\n![](images/86/8642db9fd933ec2f15c6712c713cfaec8fb20052066dcca3915904190d3551fa.png)\n\nEach point in this domain is uniquely mapped to a real number $z.$ Possible values of $z$ can be visualized on a separate axis (depicted by the diagram on the right). \n\nIf $(x,y)$ is allowed to run over the entire domain, the set of points on the $z$-axis where the values of $f(x,y)$ land is called the **range** of the function. For this function, the range is $z\\in [0,1].$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When discussing a function's domain, we usually refer to the so-called **natural domain**, the largest set of values for which the function is defined.\n\n- We can also *restrict* the domain of a function to a subset of the natural domain. For example, we might restrict the domain of $f$ to all values of $y$ in the upper-half plane. In this case, we'd write\n\n$$\nf(x,y) =  \\sqrt{1 -x^2 -y^2}, \\quad (x,y)\\in D, \\quad D = \\big\\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, x^2 + y^2 \\leq 1, \\: y\\geq 0 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThis gives a domain/range diagram that looks as follows.\n\n![](images/fb/fbab832d984232b0b3b951579e5ee2727e92c9f4bf496d4fffe82f7230cfbb14.png)\n\nIn this case, restricting the domain does not affect the range. However, in general, restricting the domain *will* affect the range." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125869 #edb/ref 1065 7035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125869 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d81a3bfc-ca0f-528c-ad48-e606581c6d6c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125869 #edb/ref 1067 "The Domain and Range of a Two-Variable Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272390 #edb/ref 1080 "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<0}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272390 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272390 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "00bacbcd-590d-557f-a9f4-6a4269e68310" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273476 #edb/ref 1080 "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-1\\le x-y\\le 1}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273476 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273476 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7ed55f36-0c59-534d-95e8-07b34cf2dc0d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273604 #edb/ref 1080 "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x-y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273604 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273604 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8cbe26d2-a7ea-57c3-b47a-0b429892c77c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273631 #edb/ref 1080 "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273631 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273631 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8f76b85c-0fb2-5e25-b12d-307a72a16bd9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273802 #edb/ref 1080 "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0\\le x-y\\le \\pi }" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273802 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273802 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a40eb757-e322-5c5a-ae24-103986e65d27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273983 #edb/ref 1080 "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-1\\le x-y\\le 1,\\,x>0}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273983 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273983 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b8517e7a-c661-562c-81e8-faa0f8fe98e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274306 #edb/ref 1080 "The domain is the entire $xy$-plane" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274306 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274306 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "db332580-91a3-58b1-ad8e-07bfc400b3ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274357 #edb/ref 1080 "The domain is the entire $xy$-plane" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274357 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274357 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "df3e244c-ae04-56cb-8d2f-103fe2a767ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274424 #edb/ref 1080 "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274424 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274424 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e6619d40-8fda-56f8-ab78-d3db56de9c51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274622 #edb/ref 1080 "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<1}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274622 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274622 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fe29f23c-2a64-564f-9465-5e972cf08268" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274793 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186273476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274793 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186273604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274793 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186273802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274793 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186273983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274793 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186274306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274793 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274793 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274793 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186273476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274793 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "5b7c962d-3d23-53a3-911a-272dffdfe63f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274851 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186272390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274851 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186273631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274851 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186274357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274851 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186274424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274851 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186274622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274851 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274851 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274851 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186274622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274851 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7d3ee636-ed6b-5d29-ac3b-8e818ea5617f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275453 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275453 #edb/ref 1069 "q-158179" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275453 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275453 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the domain of the function $z=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275453 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8022d60c-ad02-52ea-a2bc-6bcc108f5f02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275453 #edb/ref 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero, and\n\n- the expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<1}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
